in our home  communities or within the city or within  the mountains next to us  as well as food and understanding the  importance of food and healthy food how  food is grown how food systems work um  and how do they exist or not exist  within our communities as well and then  also thinking about just  other ways to blend um learning around  sciences whether that's through  literature whether that's through math  whether that's through different content  areas that teachers can connect to by  taking their students out to to the land  to be able to do that so  you know whether that's an inquiry-based  science project or it's a you know  taking a group of students to harvest  pinyon for the um for that time and  understanding why um the significance  culturally and also the significance of  pinyon within the community what that  means and then distribution like what  does that mean as well so part of that  we actually are now launching an  indigenous farmer's hub which is  building out a csa model to then like  grow food and be able to provide that  food back to to our students and  families and community at large um where  students are actively engaged in  understanding um how do you develop a  healthy food system and what does it  mean to grow harvest food and create um  like those opportunities and indigenous  farming so so that's an example um but  something i think that also connects our  students to land and to other people  within our community who may not be a  traditional teacher like in the in the  sense that they come up through um a  pipeline that might be through a  university but there are those community  members who hold all this different  knowledge and like expertise that they  can learn from  great  so  if if you think about uh teachers and  maybe a traditional public school and  where those are who want to do and bring  more of this into their classroom what  advice do you have for them about how to  think about making their classrooms more  culturally responsive  yeah i think knowing your students and  families and starting there and just i  mean and really getting to build a  relationship  when i think about being culturally like  responsive or culturally responsive in  education this is like something you  know i've spent about 18 20 months at  the state thinking about like what are  the lessons learned that we can share  um one of those like like really  understanding like what's the historical  context of the community in which you  serve um as a teacher whether you have  to do that in isolations or you can do  that as an entire school um two engaging  your community and like what it is they  want to see um and and for their  children and getting an understanding of  that so it helps guide the processes and  protocols and like outcomes that you're  you're aiming for  i also believe that like  we have to have like open conversations  and courageous conversations and we  never ever want to set up a student to  come into a classroom where they feel  like they don't belong  so the sense of belonging within a  classroom setting and or a school  setting is something that is really  really important in opening up your  curriculum so that students can explore  that in a way that's um healthy for them  and safe and also that brings in the  different dimensions of all the students  that you serve so one of our parents  reflected back to us in our first year  like when you said you were going to  serve like 62 different tribes and 13  different ethnicities and be able to do  that for your curriculum i thought it  was like like i wanted wanted to see  that i also was incredibly ambitious and  i had no idea how you were going to do  that without many different tribes and  perspectives and so we talked about like  how our  teachers were able to facilitate like  open-ended kind of universal questions  enduring understandings and essential  questions that lended themselves to  students being able to do that and when  he then and a focus group  came back and told us like my son you  know comes home and he tells me not only  did he share about where he's from and  what he knows about our family and our  culture and our background and like what  he was reading he also heard from like  the other you know  15 students in his um cohort about what  it was that um their ex family's  experience and so so then it becomes  like just a way to be able to share that  without any kind of hesitation um in and  i think that you know students now like  really understand  their own identities in ways that i  don't believe many of us ever had that  opportunity in school to be able to do  that so creating real community and  being  open ended in like how you're thinking  about teaching and also letting students  lead i think is like a good place to  start  yeah i i've  heard that community message from you  coming through loud and clear that it  really has to be  everything based on understanding  that the community that you're serving  in and and  where those are how do you if you have  new teachers coming into the school how  do you prepare them what kinds of  training do they go through  yeah so a couple of things one um you  know as we were growing the school every  year we'd have like a new set of  teachers coming in to to trying to to  that's a lot of it's just modeling so  like when we started the first day of  school it was very different um we  started with a song from one of our  navajo instructors as well as a song  from one of our lakota instructors and  it was we were outside in a circle with  families um and students at the same  time really just acknowledging like this  is very different  but this is like shouldn't be different  right it should be the norm and it is  now the norm right so so just like being  able to have um and i would always  invite um  our  new teachers to those type of  experiences before they come on board so  that they're seeing how maybe we close  the end of one year out and those  significant traditions that we have  whether it was like you know our  ceremonies at the end around student  recognition and graduation and what  those look like um  whether it was like a community um kind  of a event of some sort too whether it's  the annual powwow or feast day that we  have in the fall  um  but other than that it's like building  in the time first of all it's like  giving teachers like three hours a week  to be able to have time with their teams  having a mentor within the school  that understands the school culture core  values and what does that mean when we  talk about indigenous perspectives and  education if you're native or non-native  and you're a teacher at the school  as well as  we created a  couple of different things one is called  growing educators for native american  communities where we co-created with a  community college a certification  program that uses the naca framework a  curricular framework to redesign all of  the courses that those teachers would  take and then we took that and that's  our professional development model now  so it's grounded on the um kind of  notion of ke which is a word from the  demonation which talks about  relationships and then goes into like  the cycle that is a navajo learning  cycle  which is aligned with the four  directions which basically is about  reflection planning implementation and  then reflection again which is something  that has been in place within our  communities for a very long time  um so doing the work by using like the  different aspects of what we know to be  true um the second was the indigenous  educators corps which is an america love  americorps um which gives you know 20  some young  folks or elders to the opportunity to be  within schools and to  serve alongside educators in these  different schools that we support and  then hopefully becoming teachers  themselves but i would say by being  connected having the time to do that  and realizing that there's not a book or  a textbook you can pick up that's going  to be able to say like  that's how you like indigenize education  um eventually maybe somebody will write  that one of us should probably do that  at some point but like but working  collaboratively yeah and being able to  to um show what that's like and then  always having  many resources within the individuals  who are around our students and families  that want to see this happen so that's  always always been helpful too  great well i could talk to you all day  but we are already at the end of our  time thank you so much for sharing your  work it is  so interesting and inspiring to hear so  thank you for all you're doing  well thank you and thanks for this  opportunity huge fans of all the work  that you all do in the world too and so  i appreciate this and i hope you all  have a good um rest of your month and  day thank you  and for all those listening we do have  another show on thursday with pedro de  brucier he's the author of urban myths  about learning and education  and more urban myths about learning and  education and he will be announcing for  the first time his new book so 12 p.m  pacific 3 eastern on thursday to hear  more thanks all for joining us  you


